content.text:
Note `#creator` pointer-events:none plus `#creator, #creator * { pointer-events: none; }` then specific re-enables — but I re-enabled `.crPanel` etc at top before the wildcard... order: the `#creator, #creator *` rule comes AFTER the earlier statement, so it overrides for panels too since specificity: `#creator *` (id + *) specificity (1,0,1) vs `#creatorHead` (1,0,0)... `#creator *` computed specificity: id + universal = 1-0-0? Universal selector counts as 0. So `#creator *` = (1,0,0) same as `#creatorHead` — later rule wins → everything none. Bad. Fix: drop those first rules, and re-enable panels after the wildcard. Let me restructure: single rule `#creator * { pointer-events: none }` at top, then later `#creatorHead, .crPanel, #creatorFoot, #zoomBtns, .vbtn, #zoomBtns button, #ratName, #ratName:focus` re-enable pointer-events after. Since re-enable rules appear later with same specificity → win for those elements. But children inside .crPanel also need auto: `.crPanel *` — but `.optBtn` inside; set `.crPanel * { pointer-events: auto }`? That would re-enable everywhere inside panels — fine, since panels are the interactive area anyway. And #creatorHead * (buttons). Foot *.

Simplest: 
```
#creator { pointer-events: none; }
#creator * { pointer-events: none; }
/* interactive areas */
#creatorHead, #creatorHead *, .crPanel, .crPanel *, #zoomBtns, #zoomBtns *, #creatorFoot, #creatorFoot * { pointer-events: auto; }

